#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "cdad8b80-f85f-5555-92da-58a974f322a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-12380" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+y^2}} \\: \\textrm{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=1+x+y^2$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $-1 \\leq y \\leq 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(1+x+y^2\\right) = 2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{z-x+{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(1+x+{y}^{2})-x+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(2y)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2(1+2{y}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{1}{\\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\cancel{\\sqrt{1+2{y}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "558bc791-1cd4-51fc-9e2c-9efdd063323d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12381" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8675cb0c59ba915cefe4b0bbe539f1e7e38b5a3ac2fae4bd2bb25e4de96e116.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105459 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/id #uuid "02451ec6-38a3-5cfc-adfb-912b91fab9dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12382" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=xz,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=x^2-y$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 2$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n![](images/69/69764cda6db58d51cf12ab33a352191513d3f572292203135108bfc15f902584.svg)\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2-y) = 2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x^2-y) = -1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,x({x}^{2}-y)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,({x}^{3}-xy)\\sqrt{2+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is ${\\color{blue} (x^3-xy) \\sqrt{2+4x^2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be6bfb1-22ec-54d7-8d70-408044da5849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415944} {:answer/id #uuid "11f496da-87e3-5a3b-81b0-ad4ccd99eec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415946} {:answer/id #uuid "1ac43cfe-450e-5f00-a858-df79110cc719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415949} {:answer/id #uuid "855fc78b-5ccc-59f0-bca3-89185d1730e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415960} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c85dc7-b6d4-5404-8564-0781f52fb42d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+y+xy)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415968}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0be6bfb1-22ec-54d7-8d70-408044da5849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415944} :answer-field/id #uuid "d906cb4e-f500-54c4-87ea-509d8da51301" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f27ceb2-3e83-5aa6-8caa-7e1929889c49" :question/math-academy-id "q-161106" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=2xy,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2x,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})=2y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(2x)}^{2}+{(2y)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2xy\\sqrt{1+4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008daf1a-a433-5118-aad3-61bc2cd82151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415943} {:answer/id #uuid "11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415945} {:answer/id #uuid "31b4ae36-f11f-5826-afc4-9e1f22cf793d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415954} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac59834-bde8-5e44-938a-c936359f2042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415961} {:answer/id #uuid "a786601a-73a7-5725-8042-296bce616964" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415945} :answer-field/id #uuid "105048ab-2d03-5b31-abc9-b516f5aaa13b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c04d8b1-030c-5d18-9c3c-13b32378d108" :question/math-academy-id "q-161316" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b1b263baf01b692a0344388207c12d3c4dac0f49d11cd4bfa744f2841df2c6b.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the fourth quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =9[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1-1) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105889 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8885504f-07fb-5308-a982-608d7baa262b" :knowledge-point/title "Express Surface Integrals as Repeated Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "246b6bfe-20aa-55cc-b239-3304de81494f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415952} {:answer/id #uuid "72fa2757-f8e2-52c7-97eb-f3ea64fd0de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415959} {:answer/id #uuid "a9391385-1fbe-53da-993e-c0f38007e6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415964} {:answer/id #uuid "abd564cc-a7f0-5f60-a224-edcb8733b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415965} {:answer/id #uuid "fa49e0e8-2a63-57cc-8df5-3f02f87ef7e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd564cc-a7f0-5f60-a224-edcb8733b99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415965} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a33e921-a9bc-5edf-9d0c-b149f91622e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e3b0ae8-2464-5f3a-ba5b-28ac6b071959" :question/math-academy-id "q-161326" :question/problem "Consider the surface integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2}y,$ where the surface $S$ is defined by $z=-\\sqrt{2}x-y$ for $-1\\le x\\le 0$ and $0\\le y\\le 2.$\nThis surface integral can be written as a repeated integral, as shown below. \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{2}x-y)=-\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{2}x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{2}y\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,2\\sqrt{2}y\\,\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193323db-4837-5978-b14e-f2ac7216eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415948} {:answer/id #uuid "6a54bfb9-103b-535a-a0de-5777c5154e7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415958} {:answer/id #uuid "a40290ed-721f-5496-959a-a2ae7e6cf644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415962} {:answer/id #uuid "c8beac62-5cb9-5816-b59e-39200a1a65b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415970} {:answer/id #uuid "cb82683b-e2da-594a-840f-2f2e47c75562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a40290ed-721f-5496-959a-a2ae7e6cf644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415962} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f085d7-1a2e-5ed1-949e-eab11615591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac50dc50-55cb-5157-8205-32f49dbad931" :question/math-academy-id "q-161338" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe04241f55bae9dae231be6486a3c53985c8dc4af9e9656787267d9d852f829e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{3}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}(1-{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "137ced3c-e27e-55d0-a129-d7f6d14ddfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415947} {:answer/id #uuid "1dee489f-a3c9-5e67-9010-8a2bb6344bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415950} {:answer/id #uuid "24d68779-dfc9-5e3b-9438-879f14f3462b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415953} {:answer/id #uuid "6354a1eb-c050-5050-93ac-b70e93b7b31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415956} {:answer/id #uuid "ba006680-3290-5c38-89b3-8d6c40bc62cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "137ced3c-e27e-55d0-a129-d7f6d14ddfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415947} :answer-field/id #uuid "867991c0-ff4c-568a-b34d-41463d47a5c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44573c5a-fe32-5ee3-9d3f-c65bffe1a9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-161342" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(0)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{D}\\,2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y]_{y=0}^{y=\\pi /2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x(0-1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e34a506-206c-5a51-9251-f1cd31df64d4" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20eaebd4-47b4-5a06-b816-cc531888309e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{121}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415951} {:answer/id #uuid "3829b03c-0d7f-5b85-969e-c475546e2d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{395}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415955} {:answer/id #uuid "6a510a5b-13c1-5f24-b8c2-ff019d89d2c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415957} {:answer/id #uuid "ae2d7ec7-e4c0-578d-84f7-8e6cf8e46b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415966} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c0bc18-5385-5a20-91df-0c00a7144747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2c0bc18-5385-5a20-91df-0c00a7144747" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415967} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d471815-2895-5fb8-bed5-64d74c7a07a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4f6d27cd-0aea-5d32-8f1f-4a62535f9e3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161351" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(24xy+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=3x-4y$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-4y)=3,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x-4y)=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be evaluated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(24xy+{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,(24xy+(3x-4y)^{2})\\sqrt{1+{3}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{∬}_{D}\\,(24xy+9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-24xy)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{∬}_{D}\\,(9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(9{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[9{x}^{2}y+\\frac{16}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(9{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}{[3{x}^{3}+\\frac{16}{3}x]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{26}(24+\\frac{32}{3}-3-\\frac{16}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{79}{3}\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
